  "body": "## Objective\nMake WebGPU performance measurable at pass level without conflating CPU command encoding with GPU execution.\n\n## Scope\nOptional `timestamp-query` support; per-pass GPU timing; CPU scene-prep/encoding timing separately; frame statistics; instance/light/occluder/cluster/sample counts; DSO tile/pixel counts; secondary-effect resolutions; renderer-owned texture/buffer/history memory estimates; JSON export.\n\n## Non-goals\nNo optimization or quality reduction in this issue.\n\n## Dependencies / concurrency\nDepends on SM-102/103; should be extended as passes land. Must be complete before SM-501 and later performance claims.\n\n## Canonical context\n`docs/WEBGPU_VALIDATION_PLAN.md` §§16–17; `AGENTS.md` performance discipline.\n\n## Implementation prompt\nUse timestamp queries only when supported and label unavailable GPU timing explicitly. Do not call CPU submission time GPU time. Design instrumentation to impose bounded/known overhead and allow benchmark mode to collect stable distributions.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n- Major frame-graph passes report GPU times when supported.\n- CPU prep/encode and GPU timing are distinct fields.\n- Diagnostics/export include scene workload counts and memory estimates.\n- Missing timestamp-query path remains functional.\n\n## Verification\nCompare instrumentation on adapters with/without timestamp-query where available; sanity-check timestamp ordering and disabled-instrumentation overhead.\n\n## Expected artifacts\nTiming/query infrastructure, diagnostics panel/export schema, benchmark integration, docs.\n\n## Blocking / stopping conditions\nIf timestamp-query is unavailable on target browser/adapter, keep the issue partially hardware-blocked for GPU timing but finish non-query diagnostics; never fabricate GPU times.",
